{\*\pnseclvl1\pnucrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl2\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl3\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl4\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl5
\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl6\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl7\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl8\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang
{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright \lang1024\cgrid {\f1\lang1033 
\par }{\f1\fs32\lang1033 APR: What Is It, and Why We Use It In Apache
\par }{\f1\lang1033 Ryan Bloom
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Why Apache 2.0 Requires a Portable Run-Time
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Apache Portable Run-time (APR) is a library that provides a consistent API for standard system calls across all supported platforms.  This allows Apache 
to use native calls on all platforms while keeping the base Apache code clean and easy to follow.
\par Apache 1.3 is an extremely portable web server that works on most versions of Unix, Windows, and some mainframes.  Why then, does Apache 2.0 require the inclu
sion of a portable run-time?  The answer is actually quite simple.  Apache 1.3 was becoming harder and harder to maintain and there were platforms that weren't being taking full advantage of because their native API's weren\rquote 
t being used.  Both of these were issues when work began on Apache 2.0 and it was imperative that they were addressed correctly.
\par The Apache 1.3 Windows port was mostly hacked into the code just to make it work.  Because Apache on Windows is a threaded web server instead of a pre-forking 
server, most of the code used to manage the server itself needed to be redesigned and re-coded.  This was also the case for the creation of new processes for CGI processing.  Having code duplicated in the tree caused a couple of bug problems: bugs were ei
t
her fixed on Unix while the same bug remained on Windows or a bug would be fixed on Windows and cause an additional bug on a Unix machine.  Problems such as this could be attributed to having one function that worked differently on two different platforms
.  APR was designed to fix this problem, which was likely to get much worse in Apache 2.0, because there were plans to support more platforms that didn\rquote t use standard Unix functions such as fork.
\par The second problem to deal with was one of performance.  Apac
he has historically been a POSIX based web server, which has always made sense because the majority of operating systems that Apache had to run on provided a reliable POSIX layer. However, Apache now has to run on systems that are POSIX compliant, but in 
w
hich it is implemented very poorly.  Windows, for example, is a completely POSIX compliant platform, but most of the instabilities that have been identified on Windows are due to using POSIX calls instead of the native API's.  Using POSIX calls is also to
 blame for the performance problems that Apache on Windows suffers from.  This problem was also likely to get worse in Apache 2.0, again as a result of the plan to support additional platforms.
\par There were two options from which to choose when trying to fix 
these problems.  The first was to continue using #ifdef's to separate code for different platforms.  This had the advantage of keeping all of the code in one place, but had the disadvantage of the code being much harder to read and to modify.  It was also
 
likely that a bug fix on one platform would not be carried over to any or all of the other platforms supported by Apache when using this method.  The second option was to use a portable run-time to make all of the platforms look alike to Apache.  This opt
i
on also had advantages and disadvantages.  Using this option makes the Apache code much easier to read and understand.  It is also increases the probability that a bug fix will propagate through to all platforms.  The disadvantage is some loss of control 
in Apache.  Because it is important to ensure that all platforms can work with code written for APR, APR uses incomplete types, meaning it is not possible to get access to the platforms\rquote  native types.
\par That covers the initial reasons for wanting a portable r
un-time in Apache 2.0.  As it happens, the code is still being duplicated to handle differences between platforms.  This section of code is called the Multi-Processing Module, which is covered in more detail in the presentation, "Apache 2.0 Overview," by 
Manoj Kasichainula and in the night-school class, "Migrating Apache 1.3 Modules to Apache 2.0," by Ryan Bloom.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Why Write the Apache Portable Run-Time
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard\plain \s28\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Once the decision had been made to include a portable run-time in Apache 2.0, the next stage was to deter
mine whether to use an exisiting portable run-time or write a new one.  The first step was to evaluate currently available portable run-times.  The main options were to either use the Netscape Portability Run-Time (NSPR), the ADAPTIVE Communication Enviro
nment (ACE), or to try to convince a big company to Open-Source their existing run-time.
\par }\pard\plain \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright \lang1024\cgrid {\f1\lang1033 NSPR was an obvious choice because Dean Gaudet had already used it once to create a portable threaded version of Apache.  NSPR was well designed and had already been pr
oven to work cross-platform.  There were a few problems that kept NSPR from being used with Apache.  First, the initial license for NSPR was not compatible with the Apache license, which would have kept some of the Apache Group members from being able to 
contribute to Apache 2.0.  After a discussion of the issue, Netscape promised to modify its license to accommodate Apache\rquote 
s needs.  However, a few months passed and the release with the new license never came.  A month after work was started on APR, NSPR wa
s finally released with the compliant license.  At this point, the ASF made a technical decision not to use NSPR because it had too many features that Apache didn't require.
\par The next option was a package by the name of ACE.  However, ACE was written in C++
 not C, which basically made it useless to Apache.  The Apache Group has had a long-standing position of not using C++ in Apache in order to make it easier for new people to join the Apache effort.  The general feeling within the Apache Group is that more
 people know C than know C++ and that those who do know C++ can write C code.
\par The final option was to get a proprietary run-time library Open Sourced.  Unfortunately, while it is generally easy to find closed source run-time libraries, getting them opened is usually quite difficult.  This option was being explored in early 1999 
\endash  before most of the big companies started rushing to Open Source their code.  Because of the huge amount of red tape involved with this alternative, this course was abandoned early on.
\par After exhausting these possibilities, the Apache Group began writing its own run-time library.  At this point, the scope of APR needed to be established. APR could be limited to basic system functions or be broad enough to provide any function that is p
ortable across platforms.  Because Apache had already implemented some functions that would not be considered system functions in a portable manner, it was decided to go ahead and implement any function that was written to be portable across platforms.

\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par What Does APR Provide
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
The first version of APR only provides functions that are useful to a server application because of the importance of its inclusion in Apache 2.0.  Future versions of APR may provide additional functions that are not required by Apache
.  The following list of necessary functions was created by looking at the NSPR port that Dean created:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls1\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Status Values
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls2\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 File I/O
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls3\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls3\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Network I/O
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls4\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Critical Section Locking
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls5\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls5\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Memory Mapped Files
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls6\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Threads and Processes
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls7\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Time
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls8\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls8\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Memory Management
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls9\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls9\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Functions taken from Apache 1.3

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f4\cgrid \loch\af4\dbch\af0\hich\f4 \'95\tab}}\pard \fi-283\li1723\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf4\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'95}}\hyphpar0\ls10\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Portability Routines
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
Because most of these functions are implemented using incomplete types, application programmers can not just access internal fields for each type.  Each incomplete type therefore provides a set of routines that allow access to the
 internal fields.  For example, it is possible to retrieve a file's name by calling ap_get_filename and passing in an open file type.
\par }\pard\plain \s28\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Each APR type contains a pointer to a context (these are described in the Memory Management section) except for the time t
ypes.  The context is used for memory management and to attach user data to any APR type.
\par }\pard\plain \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright \lang1024\cgrid {\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Status Values
\par }\pard \qc\fi735\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 The status values in APR fall into four categories: }{\i\f1\lang1033 system error}{\f1\lang1033 , }{\i\f1\lang1033 error value}{\f1\lang1033 , }{\i\f1\lang1033 status value}{\f1\lang1033 , & }{
\i\f1\lang1033 success}{\f1\lang1033 .  }{\i\f1\lang1033 System errors}{\f1\lang1033  are values between zero and APR
_OS_START_ERROR.  These errors are returned when an APR function fails to complete because a native function has failed.  If a platform does not provide a particular error value, such as EAGAIN, APR will provide a value APR_EAGAIN that can be used in its 
place.  
\par APR }{\i\f1\lang1033 error values}{\f1\lang1033 
 are values between APR_OS_START_ERROR and APR_OS_START_STATUS.  This category of status codes will be returned if an APR function fails to complete for any reason that is not related to the platform.  For example, if an un-initiali
zed file is passed to ap_get_filename, APR_ENOFILE will be returned. 
\par APR }{\i\f1\lang1033 status values}{\f1\lang1033  are between APR_OS_START_STATUS and APR_OS_START_SYSERR.  This category is used when an APR function has completed successfully but has more information that it must ex
press.  For example, when ap_wait needs to inform the caller that a child process is still running, it returns APR_CHILD_NOTDONE, but when it wishes to express that a child process has finished executing, it will return APR_CHILD_DONE.
\par The }{\i\f1\lang1033 success}{\f1\lang1033  value is returned whenever an APR function completes successfully and has no other information to express.  It is represented by APR_SUCCESS.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 File I/O
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 File I/O is used whenever we are reading from or writing to a file or pipe. Because of the differences between 
platforms, this is one place where the need for APR becomes very apparent.  Files are handled very differently between Unix and Windows.  Windows provides a HANDLE type that is used for files.  Unix provides both buffered and unbuffered files, represented
 
by an integer and a FILE type respectively. These differences make it very difficult to write code that works on both platforms.  To solve this problem, APR has provided its own type ap_file_t that represents files on all supported platforms.  All of the 
r
egular file operations are supported through this type.  It is possible to open, read, write, close, delete, duplicate, and get a file's information.  It is also possible to check for end of file and get the name of a file pointed to by a specific ap_file
_t.
\par Getting a file's information is a bit different from other APR functions.  APR's file information structure is one of the few non-incomplete types provided by APR.  This is because the information it is possible to retrieve about files is pretty much th
e same across platforms.  Information not provided by some platforms is represented by an error value.  For example, on Windows the user field is always APR_ENOTIMPL (this value may be changed, or it may be implemented using ifdefs soon).  
\par File I/O is als
o used for piped communication.  As people who have followed Apache on Windows know, pipes on Windows do not always provide the same level of support that Unix pipes allow.  For example, not all pipes on Windows can be timed out.  To handle this, APR incl
udes the ability to attach a timeout value to files.  This value is meaningless on regular files, but on pipes it determines how long a program will attempt to read from or write to a file until it gives up.
\par Finally, File I/O encompasses directory access.  Directories are represented using the ap_dir_t type.  They can be opened, closed, created, read, and deleted if empty.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Network I/O
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Network I/O is used for communicating over a network.  Currently, the only transport layer supported is TCP.  This will mos
t likely change in time.  Sockets are represented using the ap_socket_t type.  It is possible to attach a timeout value to sockets to determine how long to wait while trying to read or write.  If the timeout value expires, APR_ETIMEUP will be returned.  S
o
ckets can be bound to ports by first assigning a port to the socket using ap_set_local_port and then calling ap_bind.   Once bound, sockets are ready to listen for connections, which is done using ap_listen.  APR also provides the ap_connect routine, whic
h will make a connection between any two TCP sockets.  
\par Reading and writing to sockets is accomplished using the ap_send and ap_recv routines.  There has been some work done to implement ap_sendfile.  If APR supports sendfile on a particular platform, the A
PR_HAS_SENDFILE feature macro will be defined to be TRUE.
\par Network I/O also incorporates polling sockets.  This is achieved through the use of the ap_poll routines.  These routines cannot be used to poll pipes or files, although the Apache Group hopes to ev
entually incorporate this ability.  To set up a polling structure, a user must first call ap_setup_poll, then add the sockets to the poll structure by calling ap_add_poll_socket.  To poll the current set of sockets, ap_poll should be called.  It is then p
o
ssible to check the results by calling ap_get_revents for each socket in the set.  At some point in the future, APR will be modified to include a function that will return a list of sockets that were affected by the poll.  Finally, t is possible to remove
 individual sockets from a poll set and to clear the results of the previous poll in anticipation of the next call to ap_poll.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Critical Section Locking
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
\par Critical sections are those sections of code that should only have either one thread or one process in 
them at any one time.  This is accomplished using the ap_lock_t type.  There are three ways to lock a section of code: APR_CROSS_PROCESS, APR_INTRAPROCESS, and APR_LOCKALL.  An APR_CROSS_PROCESS lock is guaranteed to never allow more than one process into
 
the affected section of code.  This is useful if a platform suffers from thundering herd problems (waking up all processes waiting on a socket when a connection is established).  It is important to note that this type of lock may or may not lock out threa
d
s as well as processes depending on the platform.  An APR_INTRAPROCESS lock is basically a thread lock.  This lock will guarantee that a section of code has only one thread in it at any given time.  Finally, an APR_LOCKALL lock will protect a critical sec
t
ion from other threads and other processes.  This is the safest kind of lock to use, because it guarantees that no other execution primitive will be allowed into the critical section.  This is also usually a heavier weight lock than APR_CROSS_PROCESS, and
 should only be used if it is really what is needed.  For example, if a program never spawns threads this lock should not be used because it is more expensive than a simple APR_CROSS_PROCESS.
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Memory Mapped Files
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Memory mapped files in APR are represented 
by ap_mmap_t types.  Although memory mapped files are sometimes used as shared memory on certain platforms, ap_mmap_t types are not suitable for this use.  APR has only implemented a very small subset of mmap'ed file uses because it needed to remain porta
ble.  APR's mmaped files can only be created, deleted, and read from.  In order to create an mmap type, the file must be represented by an APR file type.  If APR supports mmap'ed files on a platform the APR_HAS_MMAP feature macro will be defined as TRUE.

\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Threads and Processes
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
Threads are represented in APR by ap_thread_t types.  It is possible to create and destroy threads.  Threads can be created in either detached or non-detached states.  If a thread is detached, then the resources it uses will be freed 
immediately when it dies. However, this will keep other threads in the same process from knowing when the thread has terminated.  If the thread is not detached, then there must be another thread in the same process which is joined on that thread.  This jo
i
ned thread is then notified when the other thread has finished executing.  It is possible to detach a currently running thread by calling ap_detach.  It is also possible to kill a currently running thread.  This is not recommended though because on most p
latforms, processes leak memory or become unstable when one of their threads are terminated.
\par Data can also be attached to currently running threads.  This is commonly referred to as thread-local storage or thread-private storage.  This data is represented a
s ap_threadkey_t types in APR.  It is possible to attach data to a thread and retrieve that data.  The data is associated with a key value that is used for retrieval.
\par Processes can also be created in APR.  APR does provide an ap_fork function but it is not
 portable.  If your platform provides an ap_fork function, then the APR feature macro APR_HAS_FORK is defined to be TRUE.  The more portable method of creating a new process is ap_create_process.  The difference between the two is that ap_fork creates a n
ew process and starts that process at the same place in the code whereas ap_create_process creates a new process and executes a new program.  It is possible when using ap_create_process to setup up to three pipes to be used as stdin, stdout, and stderr.  

\par T
here are two more operations for processes.  The first is determining if a child process has terminated.  This function returns either APR_CHILD_DONE or APR_CHILD_NOTDONE.  In the second operation the user can send a signal to another process.  Currently,
 the only signal really supported is the signal to terminate the process.  It is very possible that the ap_kill function will be redefined to only allow this signal in the near future.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033  }{\b\f1\lang1033 Time
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 Time is the only APR category that does not use any incomplete t
ypes.  There are two different time types in APR.  The first is ap_time_t, which is the number of microseconds since the epoch, defined as 00:00:00 January 1, 1970 UTC.  The time library also has one of the only functions that doesn\rquote 
t return a status value
.  This function is ap_now, which returns the current time in ap_time_t format.  This function should be used like a thread-safe global variable.  The second time type is ap_exploded_time_t.  This is a broken down representation of the time value.  There 
a
re functions to convert between the two time types, as well as a couple of functions to convert the time value to a character string.  The first two functions to convert to a string use a common string format.  The first, ap_rfc822_date, uses the string f
o
rmat specified by RFC 822.  The second, ap_ctime, uses the same format as the POSIX function ctime.  Finally, there is a function to write the time in a user-defined format, ap_strftime, which uses the same format specifiers as the POSIX strftime function
.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Memory Management
\par }\pard \qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
Memory management is implemented using contexts in APR.  Contexts contain a pointer to memory pools, a pointer for user data, and a function pointer.  The memory pools do not require the programmer to explicitly free the memory.  All m
emory is allocated using either ap_palloc or ap_pcalloc.  When all of the memory in a specific pool is no longer needed, it is all freed using either ap_clear_context or ap_destroy_context.  It is also possible to create a sub-context by calling ap_create
_
context and supplying a non-NULL context.  Anytime memory is allocated out of a pool, it is possible to register a function to be used to cleanup the memory when the pool is freed.  The user data is associated with a key and can be set and retrieved.  It 
i
s currently not possible to de-associate user data, but it will automatically be freed when the pool is destroyed.  Lastly, the function pointer is used when an error occurs while allocating memory.  If the function is NULL, then the memory allocation rou
t
ine will return an error condition.  If it is not NULL, the function is executed.  This ability to register a function has been provided to allow backward compatibility with Apache 1.3.  In Apache 1.3, if memory allocation failed Apache quit immediately a
nd output an error string.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Functions Taken from Apache 1.3
\par 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
There is a set of functions taken directly from the Apache 1.3 ap library, including tables, getopt, ap_snprintf, ap_cpystrn, and routines for MD5 encryption. Because they were included in Apache 1.3, these functions will not be discussed in detail here.

\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Portability Routines
\par 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 One of the great truths that APR developers have had to live with is that not all programs currently use APR (hopefully that will change one day).  This is driven home when d
ealing with Apache, mod_php, and mod_perl.  Mod_php and mod_perl are two of the biggest modules used with Apache; however, neither of these modules is likely to move to APR anytime soon.  Despite this, it important to be sure that they will work with Apac
h
e 2.0.  If Apache requires APR, then it must be easy for programmers to go from APR to native types.  To accommodate this, there are a set of functions named ap_get_os_* and ap_put_os_*.  These two functions convert from APR to native types and back respe
ctively.  These are very stable functions, but they take time to perform, and for this reason they should only be used when transitioning from an APR program to one which doesn't use APR.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 APR Success Stories
\par 
\par }\pard \fi705\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 APR has already proven itself as a viable libra
ry.  ApacheBench is a benchmarking tool that has always come with Apache.  The problem is that ApacheBench historically only worked on Unix systems.  There has been some work recently to port ApacheBench to Windows, but the Windows modifications were very
 
intrusive when reading the code.  While APR was in development, it was used to easily port ApacheBench to every APR supported program.  ApacheBench now works on Unix, Windows, OS/2, and BeOS.  The changes made to ApacheBench are minimal and have little to
 no effect on the readability of the code.
\par APR has also been used to port htpasswd, a utility used to create Apache password files.  One final success story is htdigest, a utility for manipulating digest password files.  The Apache Group is currently in the
 process of porting suexec, logresolve, and rotatelogs to APR.  These are all programs from the Apache support directory. It is projected that some non-Apache-related programs will be ported to APR after Apache 2.0 is released.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\b\f1\lang1033 Future Work in APR
\par 
\par }\pard \fi720\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\adjustright {\f1\lang1033 APR ver
sion 1.0 is very close to being finished.  However, there is still much that can be done to make APR more attractive to people who are writing programs that need to work on multiple platforms.  The first thing that must be done is to add shared memory sup
p
ort.  APR will most likely use Ralf Engelschall's MM library to provide some of this support, but support for non-forking platforms will still need to be added.  Work also needs to be done on the configuration system so that different platforms can create
 tailored versions of APR.  For example, if Apache 2.0 is being run with the pre-forking MPM, then APR should be configured without threading support.  
\par Error handling also needs to be improved in APR.  Currently, if a platform returns an error value, that 
error condition can only be expressed in terms of a number.  The ability to be able to create character strings from that number is needed in order to make APR a real option for programs.
\par APR also need to be tested on more platforms.  APR has thus far been
 tested on BeOS, Windows, OS/2, Linux, AIX, FreeBSD, and Digital Unix.  This is a very small number of platforms in comparison to the number of platforms that Apache currently runs on.  Every APR library has a corresponding test program.  The more platfor
ms which validate that the tests always work, the better APR will become.
\par Finally, APR needs to be publicized.  APR is a very robust and flexible tool, but that doesn't do anybody any good unless large numbers of programmers know about it.  Having Apache 2.
0 rely on APR will go a long way towards getting its name out to other programmers, but that is not enough.  There has been talk of getting some bigger companies to use APR internally in some of their projects.  With the Apache Software Foundation expandi
ng into ever more projects, it is hoped that more ASF projects will choose to use APR to make their code easier to read and easier to port to more platforms.
\par }}